plan = "1) Iterate over each document in context. 2) For each, ask a sub-LLM to extract whether it mentions a scientist matching the given biographical and research clues, plus any 2016 paper abstract details about an excavation and a radiocarbon age. Also extract any acknowledgments (2011–2013) where the scientist was thanked in a seven-author paper about silencing a gene from a newly discovered class of molecule. 3) Aggregate these extractions. 4) Ask a sub-LLM to decide the best match and return the radiocarbon age (in years) from the 2016 paper’s abstract and the full name of the scientist, formatted concisely."

extractions = ""
i = 0
for d in context:
    i = i + 1
    r = llm_query("You will be given one document. Extract if it contains information about a specific scientist matching the following clues. If it does, provide a structured extraction; otherwise say 'No relevant info'.\n\nClues to match (any/all that apply; be explicit about which are supported by quotes from the document):\n- The person is a scientist who obtained two degrees between 1971 and 1982, both at the same university located in the Americas (American continent).\n- Since around 1984, they were associated with another South American university, working as a researcher, and since 1984 also as a professor.\n- They have a daughter named Julieta.\n- Their research lines include: (a) a molecule induced by developmental processes and abiotic stresses (not induced by pathogens) — e.g., late embryogenesis abundant (LEA) proteins; (b) planned removal of organisms' components — e.g., abscission; (c) the description of a group of organisms that can live in an uncommon place for its kind.\n- They were thanked for technical assistance in the acknowledgments section of a paper published between 2011 and 2013 by seven authors, discussing the effects of silencing a gene belonging to a newly discovered class of molecule (e.g., noncoding RNAs, etc.).\n- They published a paper in 2016 whose abstract mentions an excavation and reports a radiocarbon age (in years) for objects found.\n\nFor this document, do the following:\n1) Identify any scientist(s) and provide their full name(s).\n2) Provide evidence quotes from the document for each relevant clue above (degrees with years and university; association since 1984; daughter Julieta; research lines; acknowledgments detail; 2016 paper with excavation and radiocarbon age in years). Include exact numbers and names as quoted.\n3) If the 2016 paper abstract is present or referenced, extract the radiocarbon age in years of the objects found in the excavation and restate it plainly as: 'Radiocarbon age (years): <number and unit as given>'.\n4) If the acknowledgments (2011–2013, seven authors, gene silencing, newly discovered class of molecule) are present, list the paper title, year, author list count, and the acknowledgment line mentioning the scientist.\n\nDocument begins:\n-----\n" + d + "\n-----\nPlease output in this template:\n[Scientist]\nName: <full name>\nDegrees: <details>\nAffiliations: <details>\nFamily: <details>\nResearch lines: <details>\nAcknowledgments (2011–2013 gene-silencing paper): <details>\n2016 paper (excavation): <details>\nRadiocarbon age (years): <value or 'not found'>\nQuotes: <verbatim supporting quotes>\n\nIf no relevant info, output: No relevant info.")
    extractions = extractions + "\n\n=== Document " + str(i) + " Extraction ===\n" + r

final_query = "We need to answer the following question concisely:\n- A scientist got two degrees between 1971 and 1982 at the same university in the Americas. Since 1984, they were associated with another South American university as a researcher and since 1984 also as a professor. They have a daughter Julieta. Their research lines include: a molecule induced by developmental processes and stresses not induced by pathogens (e.g., LEA proteins), planned removal of organisms' components (e.g., abscission), and the description of a group of organisms that can live in an uncommon place for its kind. They were thanked for technical assistance in a paper published between 2011 and 2013 by seven authors discussing effects of silencing a gene from a newly discovered class of molecule. What is the age in years (radiocarbon) of the objects found in an excavation mentioned in the abstract of a paper they published in 2016, and what is the full name of the scientist?\n\nHere are extractions from the context documents:\n" + extractions + "\n\nInstructions:\n1) Identify the single scientist that best matches all the biographical and research clues (degrees 1971–1982 at same university; associated since 1984 with a South American university as researcher and professor; daughter Julieta; research on LEA proteins, abscission, and description of organisms living in an uncommon place; acknowledged 2011–2013 seven-author gene-silencing paper).\n2) From that scientist’s 2016 paper, extract the radiocarbon age in years of the objects found in an excavation as stated in the abstract.\n3) Output ONLY one line in the format: <AGE IN YEARS> ; <FULL NAME>\n- Use the age exactly as given (e.g., '4100 years BP', '1200 years', etc.).\n- Do not add any extra words or punctuation."

answer = llm_query(final_query)
return_result(answer)